\description{
For every observed step, draws \code{n_control} random (control) steps from a
movement kernel estimated from the observed step lengths and turning
angles, and stores the density of every step under that kernel. Three
estimators of the movement kernel are available: independent gamma and von
Mises distributions (\code{"traditional"}), a bivariate kernel density estimate
(\code{"density"}), and a random forest density surface sampled with MCMC
(\code{"density_advanced"}). The stored densities allow correcting the
likelihood of step selection models for non-uniform sampling of random
steps, e.g. in \code{\link[=dnn_ssf]{dnn_ssf()}} and \code{\link[=cnn_ssf]{cnn_ssf()}}.
}
\details{
\strong{Estimators.}
\itemize{
\item \code{"traditional"}: random steps are generated with \code{\link[amt:random_steps]{amt::random_steps()}},
which fits a gamma distribution to step lengths and a von Mises
distribution to turning angles. \code{density} is the product of both fitted
densities, i.e. step length and turning angle are assumed to be
independent.
\item \code{"density"}: a bivariate Gaussian kernel density estimate of step length
and turning angle is fitted with \code{\link[ks:kde]{ks::kde()}} (plug-in bandwidth,
\code{\link[ks:Hpi]{ks::Hpi()}}), and random steps are drawn from it with \code{\link[ks:rkde]{ks::rkde()}}
(smoothed bootstrap). Draws with a step length of zero or below, or a
turning angle outside -pi to pi, are discarded. Unlike the traditional
approach, this captures correlations between step length and turning
angle and multimodal distributions. Turning angles are treated as linear,
not circular, so densities close to -pi and pi are slightly
underestimated.
\item \code{"density_advanced"}: a random forest is trained on binned counts of
observed step lengths and turning angles, normalised by Monte Carlo
integration, and sampled with MCMC (\pkg{BayesianTools}). Unlike the
kernel density estimate, the surface is not pulled towards zero at the
edges of the observed range. Densities are bounded below by \code{1e-10}, so
that their logarithm is always finite.
}

For both density-based estimators, step lengths are considered between 0
and 1.5 times the longest observed step.

\strong{Using the densities.} Random steps drawn from the movement kernel are not
uniformly distributed in space. Subtracting \code{log(density)} from the linear
predictor of each step before normalising within strata
(importance-sampling correction) accounts for this. \code{\link[=dnn_ssf]{dnn_ssf()}} reads the
column \code{density} automatically; for \code{\link[=cnn_ssf]{cnn_ssf()}}, pass it as
\code{density = x$density}. To fit without the correction, remove the column
before calling \code{\link[=dnn_ssf]{dnn_ssf()}}.

\strong{Internal helpers} (only used for \code{"density_advanced"}), defined at the
top of the function body:
\itemize{
\item \code{fitDensitySurfaceRF()} bins the observed (step length, turning angle)
pairs onto a grid and trains a random forest that predicts the bin
counts.
\item \code{evalDensitySurfaceRF()} evaluates the fitted (unnormalised) surface at
arbitrary points, applying the Jacobian correction required for
\code{sl_transform = "log1p"}.
\item \code{estimateNormalizingConstantRF()} estimates, by Monte Carlo integration,
the constant that turns the surface into a density integrating to 1.
\item \code{bayesianToolsSampleDensitySurface()} draws new (step length, turning
angle) pairs from the normalised surface with the \code{"DEzs"} sampler of
\code{\link[BayesianTools:runMCMC]{BayesianTools::runMCMC()}}.
